     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied reference semantics and the proof-local mathematical
     6	definitions in `verification.k`, the following partial-correctness theorem is
     7	machine checked for every K `Int` value `N`:
     8	
     9	> Calling the exact translated `even_odd_count` closure with `N` returns a
    10	> two-element tuple whose first component is the number of even decimal digits
    11	> of `abs(N)` and whose second component is the number of odd decimal digits.
    12	> Zero has the one-digit representation `"0"`, hence returns `(1, 0)`.
    13	
    14	The theorem includes name lookup, argument evaluation, the `abs` builtin,
    15	function-frame creation, parameter binding, the exact program body, return
    16	control, frame removal, and restoration of caller state. It is a
    17	partial-correctness result; termination is not a formal conclusion.
    18	
    19	## Formal claim and scope
    20	
    21	`SPEC.even-odd-count` in `spec.k` starts from:
    22	
    23	- `<k> Call(Name("even_odd_count"), N:Int) </k>`;
    24	- module binding `"even_odd_count" |-> evenOddClosure`;
    25	- the supplied builtins scope;
    26	- empty heap and stack, module environment 0, `noRet`, `NoExc`, and exit code 0.
    27	
    28	It ends in `tuple(vCons(?ER, vCons(?OR, .ValSeq)))`, with:
    29	
    30	```text
    31	?ER ==Int decEven(N)
    32	?OR ==Int decOdd(N)
    33	```
    34	
    35	The formal domain is all unbounded mathematical integers (`N:Int`), with no
    36	sign or magnitude restriction. The result tuple is the observable result. The
    37	final environment, scopes, heap, stack, return state, exception state, and exit
    38	code are also constrained by the claim.
    39	
    40	For positive `n`, the proof summaries use:
    41	
    42	```text
    43	q = (n - pyMod(n, 10)) /Int 10
    44	p = pyMod(n, 2)
    45	
    46	evenPos(0) = 0
    47	oddPos(0)  = 0
    48	evenPos(n) = 1 - p + evenPos(q)
    49	oddPos(n)  = p     + oddPos(q)
    50	```
    51	
    52	This is decimal digit removal by division by 10. A digit has the same parity as
    53	the represented integer, so `p` is precisely the oddness of the removed final
    54	digit. `decEven` and `decOdd` add the special representation of zero and apply
    55	the positive summaries to the magnitude of negative inputs.
    56	
